From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: grimtickle-overlay <overlay@localhost>
Date: Sat Oct 31 12:30:00 2026 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] pci: sky1-acpi: name synthetic platform devices after the vendor driver

The ACPI scan handler creates its filtered platform devices with the
name "sky1-pcie".  That is also the exact driver-name of the mainline
pci-sky1.c host driver now shipped in Linux 7.3, so platform_match()'s
last-resort name comparison can bind those devices to the wrong driver
before the vendor pci-sky1-cix driver gets a chance (the mainline driver
uses PROBE_PREFER_ASYNCHRONOUS, which makes the outcome depend on
initcall/probe ordering).

The mainline probe then fails on the missing DT-only named resources
with a confusing -ENODEV, or -- if the CIX resource-name injection has
already run -- could partially claim the same controller.

Name the synthetic device "sky1-pcie-cix", matching the vendor driver's
name and ACPI HID binding, so the two drivers can never collide by name.

Signed-off-by: grimtickle-overlay <overlay@localhost>
---
 0184-pci-sky1-acpi-name-pdev-after-vendor-driver.patch | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertions(+), 1 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/pci/controller/cadence/pci-sky1-acpi.c
+++ b/drivers/pci/controller/cadence/pci-sky1-acpi.c
@@ -100,7 +100,7 @@
 	 * The ACPI companion is bound post-registration (see below) so
 	 * bus_find_device_by_acpi_dev() works for RSTL reset lookups.
 	 */
-	pdevinfo.name = "sky1-pcie";
+	pdevinfo.name = "sky1-pcie-cix";
 	pdevinfo.id = PLATFORM_DEVID_AUTO;
 	pdevinfo.res = resources;
 	pdevinfo.num_res = count;
